import Docs from '../../layouts/Docs.astro';
import LoadingDiagram from '../../components/LoadingDiagram.astro';
import { bundles, stats } from '../../lib/catalog';
import { scope } from '../../lib/seats';

---

<Docs
  title="Concepts"
  description="What skills, agents, bundles and harnesses are, how they load, and how they fit together."
  intro="Four words do most of the work in this catalog: skill, agent, bundle, harness. Here is each one, opened up, with real files from the catalog."
>
  <h2>Skills</h2>
  <p>
    A skill is a folder. Inside is a <code>SKILL.md</code> whose frontmatter says what the skill is for and when to use it,
    followed by the instructions themselves. Larger skills split detail into files the agent opens only when needed.
  </p>
  <pre class="tree"><code>frontend-failure-modes/
  SKILL.md                  the label and the routing table
  references/
    accessibility.md        opened when the change is interactive
    forms-and-input.md      opened when the change has a form
    performance.md
    …</code></pre>
  <pre class="file"><code>---
name: frontend-failure-modes
description: Failure-mode checklists for frontend implementation work,
  split by domain. Use when implementing or reviewing frontend changes
  that touch component state and rendering, data fetching, accessibility,
  performance, forms… Read only the reference files whose triggers match.
---

# Frontend failure modes

This skill is a router: match the change against the trigger table,
read only the files that fire, and treat every checklist item in them
as a gate.</code></pre>
  <p>
    The <code>description</code> is the most important line in the file. It is the only part the agent sees until the task
    matches, so it has to say when to reach for the skill, not just what the skill is about.
  </p>
  <LoadingDiagram />

  <h3>Global and project skills</h3>
  <p>
    Some skills are useful in every repository: triage, commit, ship, review, the teammate skills. Those are marked global in
    the catalog and <code>kura sync</code> links them once for the whole machine. Everything else is per project, chosen in
    that project's <code>kura.json</code>, so a Swift repo never sees the Terraform skills.
  </p>
  <h3>Dependencies</h3>
  <p>
    A skill can depend on others. <code>teammate</code> brings <code>how</code>, <code>why</code> and <code>teach</code>;
    <code>to-rfc</code> brings research and grilling. kura installs the whole closure. A few skills are marked dependency-only:
    they never install alone, because they only make sense as part of something bigger, and you never call them yourself.
    You ask the skill that brings them: <code>/teammate</code>, not <code>/teach</code>, <code>/how</code> or <code>/why</code>.
    The catalog marks each one with the skills it arrives through.
  </p>

  <h2>Agents</h2>
  <p>
    A skill changes what your current agent knows. An agent is a separate worker your agent hands a job to. It starts with an
    empty context, gets only the tools its file allows, follows its own rules, and returns a report. That separation is the
    point: the reviewer cannot edit what it reviews, and a seat working on the database never sees the CSS.
  </p>
  <pre class="file"><code>---
name: frontend-staff-engineer
description: Staff-level frontend implementation specialist. Use PROACTIVELY
  when delegating frontend implementation work… returns a structured
  completion report.
model: opus
effort: xhigh
memory: project
---</code></pre>
  <p>Every staff seat ends its work with the same report, so whoever dispatched it can act without rereading the diff:</p>
  <pre class="file"><code>## Completion Report: invoice CSV export (frontend slice)
**Status:** done | blocked | needs-decision
**Skills used:** frontend-failure-modes, react-router
**Gaps:** kura add tanstack-router-best-practices --type skill</code></pre>
  <p>
    There are {stats.agents} agents. Six stand alone and are useful everywhere: <code>architect</code>, <code>ux-shaper</code>, <code>code-reviewer</code>, <code>decision-judge</code>,
    and two reviewers for your harness
    setup itself. The rest arrive in bundles.
  </p>

  <h2>Bundles</h2>
  <p>
    A bundle is a seat and the standard it is held to. Sixteen bundles pair one staff-engineer agent with one failure-modes
    skill: a trigger table pointing at checklists, where every item ends in a <code>Check:</code> the seat must clear before
    reporting done. The seventeenth, <code>product-team</code>, is a whole product pipeline.
  </p>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Bundle</th><th>What the seat owns</th><th>Ships</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      {
        bundles.map((bundle) => (
          <tr>
            <td><code>{bundle.name}</code></td>
            <td>{scope[bundle.name] ?? bundle.description}</td>
            <td>
              {bundle.agents.length} {bundle.agents.length === 1 ? 'agent' : 'agents'}, {bundle.skills.length}{' '}
              {bundle.skills.length === 1 ? 'skill' : 'skills'}
            </td>
          </tr>
        ))
      }
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    Every seat has three lists of things it may do: always, only after asking, and never. Adding a dependency, changing build
    config or breaking a shared API are ask-first. Deploying, applying infrastructure or pushing to a store are never.
  </p>

  <h2>Harnesses</h2>
  <p>
    The harness is the program that runs the model: Claude Code, Pi, or anything else that reads the Agent Skills format.
    Skills are plain Markdown and travel between them; any skill folder can be copied into any harness's skills directory and
    it works. What differs is where each harness looks and what it supports. kura knows both layouts for Claude Code and Pi.
  </p>
  <p class="note">
    A few features are stronger in Claude Code than in Pi today. Seats get git-worktree isolation only in Claude Code, and the
    code-reviewer's tool allowlist is enforced there and not under Pi.
  </p>

  <h2>Decisions that wait for a person</h2>
  <p>
    The catalog splits choices into two kinds. Technique is the agent's to decide: how to structure a test, which hook to use.
    Some choices are always routed to a person, whatever any skill says:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>adding a dependency, an animation or icon library, or a hosted font,</li>
    <li>a new step on a design scale: spacing, type, color, motion, breakpoints,</li>
    <li>a change to the brand: palette, typefaces, logo,</li>
    <li>a raw value where the project already has a token,</li>
    <li>a breaking change to a shared or published API.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    When a seat meets one, it stops with <code>needs-decision</code> and a recommendation. Being right is what the
    recommendation is for; it is never a reason to go ahead.
  </p>
</Docs>

